# `SUBJECT-VOCABULARY-SCOPE`/`SCENE-KEYWORD-MEMBERSHIP`: `statechart` declares NO subject vocabulary at all,
# and the EMPTY declaration is the point — `state` + `transition` + core +
# layout + styling + the region openers is now the whole of what a statechart
# document may write at top level. There is therefore no KEEP fixture for
# this genre: what it keeps is pinned by `230-statechart-header` and
# `233-statechart-state-transition`, and this file is all five withdrawn
# cells, each with the ground of its own cell:
#
#   group      UML's grouping construct is the COMPOSITE STATE and its
#              REGIONS; under the single-source-vocabulary rule a statechart
#              that needed grouping takes UML 2.5.1 §14's word for it, not
#              another genre's.
#   external   additionally RESERVED (`RESERVED-SPELLINGS`): UML 2.5.1 §14 defines
#              TransitionKind as `external | internal | local`, so in this
#              genre's own source standard the word already names A
#              TRANSITION THAT EXITS AND RE-ENTERS ITS SOURCE STATE.
#              FigDown's means an endpoint outside the figure that is never
#              drawn — same word, same genre, same standard, unrelated
#              meanings. It may only ever come back meaning what UML means.
#   threshold  a state's box is sized by its label, so a reference value
#              drawn 60% down it asserts nothing.
#   band       a range over that same meaningless extent.
#   bundle     an ANTI-FEATURE: two transitions between the same pair of
#              states are two different TRIGGERS, and the trigger is the
#              whole content of the arc.
#
# Three authored statechart figures, all transcribed from RFCs, reach for
# none of the five.
figdown 0.2 statechart
state idle "IDLE"
group g "G"
external x "X"
threshold "Budget" 60% in=idle
band "Window" 10..20% in=idle
bundle par idle--idle
